When you send a request to the Kubernetes API (like creating a Pod, Deployment, or Service), that request passes through several stages before it’s accepted and written to etcd.

In Kubernetes, an Admission Controller is a component that intercepts API requests after authentication and authorization but before the object is stored in etcd.

kubectl apply/Create ---> API Server(Authentication and Authorization) ---> Admission Controllers ---> etcd

kubectl
   ↓
API Server
   ↓
Authentication
   ↓
Authorization
   ↓
Admission Controllers
   ↓
etcd



Authentication = Who are you?
Authorization = Are you allowed to do this?
Admission Controller = SHould this object be modified or rejected?

The role of Admission Controllers
----------------------------------
They act as gatekeepers for the Kubernetes API.
“Admission Controllers act like security and policy checkpoints inside the Kubernetes API Server.”

They can:

Validate requests (check if something is allowed)
-----------------
Mutate requests (automatically add/change fields)
---------------
Reject requests (if they break rules)
---------------

Types of Admission Controllers
------------------------------
Kubernetes has two types:  Built-in Admission Controllers and Dynamic Admission Controllers (Webhooks)


Built-in Admission Controllers: (e.g., NamespaceLifecycle, ResourceQuota, LimitRanger) — enabled in API Server.
----------------------------
COMMON BUILT-IN ADMISSION CONTROLLERS

| Controller                 | Purpose              |
| -------------------------- | -------------------- |
| NamespaceLifecycle         | Namespace validation |
| LimitRanger                | Resource limits      |
| ResourceQuota              | Namespace quotas     |
| PodSecurity                | Security policies    |
| DefaultStorageClass        | Default storage      |

------------------------------


Dynamic Admission Controllers (Webhooks) : You can write your own and register via Kubernetes API.
----------------------------------------


| Concept                  | Description                                        |
| ------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------- |
| **MutatingWebhook**      | Modifies objects (e.g., adds labels)               |
| **ValidatingWebhook**    | Enforces policies (e.g., block requests)           |
| **Common tools**         | OPA Gatekeeper, Kyverno, Istio, etc.               |

| Tool           | Purpose                  |
| -------------- | ------------------------ |
| Kyverno        | Kubernetes policy engine |
| OPA Gatekeeper | Governance policies      |
| Istio          | Sidecar injection        |
| Falco          | Runtime security         |


LAB 1 — LimitRanger Admission Controller
=======================================

Automatically apply CPU limits to containers.

kubectl create namespace dev   [Create Namespace]

Create LimitRange
------------

vi limitrange.yml

apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: cpu-limit-range
  namespace: dev
spec:
  limits:
  - default:
      cpu: 500m
      memory: 512Mi
    defaultRequest:
      cpu: 200m
      memory: 256Mi
    type: Container


| Field          | Meaning                      |
| -------------- | ---------------------------- |
| defaultRequest | Minimum guaranteed resources |
| default        | Maximum allowed usage limit  |


When pod DOES NOT specify requests,
Kubernetes automatically assigns:
CPU Request = 200m
Memory Request = 256Mi


What is a Request? Guaranteed minimum resources, Scheduler uses requests while placing pods on nodes.
What is a Limit? Maximum resources container can consume, Container cannot exceed this.

| Resource | Guaranteed | Maximum |
| -------- | ---------- | ------- |
| CPU      | 200m       | 500m    |
| Memory   | 256Mi      | 512Mi   |



kubectl apply -f limitrange.yml

Create Pod WITHOUT Limits
---------------------

vi pod.yml

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: nginx
  namespace: dev
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx

kubectl apply -f pod.yml

Verify Mutation
-------------
kubectl get pod nginx -n dev -o yaml

you will see
resources:
      limits:
        cpu: 500m
        memory: 512Mi
      requests:
        cpu: 200m
        memory: 256Mi


Admission Controller automatically injected limits.

kubectl get pods -n dev
kubectl describe pod nginx -n dev

kubectl delete pod nginx
kubectl delete ns dev




LAB 2 — ResourceQuota Admission Controller
========================================

Restrict namespace to maximum 2 pods.

kubectl create namespace dev   [Create Namespace]

Create ResourceQuota
-------------------

vi quota.yml

apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: pod-quota
  namespace: dev
spec:
  hard:
    pods: "2"

kubectl apply -f quota.yml

Create first pod: kubectl run pod1 --image=nginx -n dev
Create second pod: kubectl run pod2 --image=nginx -n dev
Create Third Pod (FAILS) : kubectl run pod3 --image=nginx -n dev
error: Error from server (Forbidden): exceeded quota

kubectl delete pod pod1 -n dev
kubectl delete pod pod2 -n dev
kubectl delete ns dev  [or directly delete the ns without deleting pods]

LAB 3 — Pod Security Admission Controller
================================
Block privileged containers.

What is Pod Security Admission?
------------------------------
Pod Security Admission is a built-in admission controller used to enforce security standards on pods.

It can:
------
block privileged containers
prevent root access
restrict dangerous capabilities
improve cluster security

Security Levels
----------------
Kubernetes provides 3 levels:
| Level      | Meaning         |
| ---------- | --------------- |
| privileged | No restrictions |
| baseline   | Basic security  |
| restricted | Strict security |

Goal of This Lab: we will use restricted , production/bank/enterprise style
----------------
We will:

Create namespace
Enable restricted security policy
Try creating insecure pod
Kubernetes blocks it automatically

kubectl apply
      ↓
API Server
      ↓
Pod Security Admission Controller
      ↓
ALLOW or DENY

Create Namespace
--------------
kubectl create namespace secure-ns
kubectl get ns

Enable Pod Security Admission
----------------
kubectl label ns secure-ns pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted

Now Kubernetes will enforce:restricted security policy inside this namespace.

Verify Label
----------

kubectl get ns secure-ns --show-labels
expected result
---------
pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted

Create Insecure Pod
-------------------

vi insecurepod.yml

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: insecure-pod
  namespace: secure-ns
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx
    securityContext:
      privileged: true


privileged: true , gives container
host-level access
elevated permissions
almost root-like machine control

Very dangerous in production.


kubectl apply -f insecurepod.yml

Kubernetes blocks it. Error from server (Forbidden): violates PodSecurity "restricted"

kubectl apply/create
      ↓
API Server receives request
      ↓
Pod Security Admission Controller checks policy
      ↓
Finds privileged=true
      ↓
Rejects pod creation


Create Secure Pod
------------------

vi securepod.yml

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: secure-pod
  namespace: secure-ns
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx

kubectl apply -f securepod.yml

kubectl get pods -n secure-ns

secure-pod   crash ? why? as per security reasons it will not create , you need to clearly specify all the rules while creating pod because you have enabled POD security admission controller

Now modify yml file, and also use unprivileged image

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: secure-pod
  namespace: secure-ns
spec:
  securityContext:
    runAsNonRoot: true
    runAsUser: 1000
    seccompProfile:
      type: RuntimeDefault

  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged
    securityContext:
      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
      capabilities:
        drop:
        - ALL

kubectl create -f secure.yml

kubectl get pods -n secure-ns

kubectl exec -it secure-pod -n secure-ns -- /bin/bash


runAsNonRoot: true / Container must NOT run as root (UID 0) : Prevents:root access inside container and privilege escalation attacks
runAsUser: 1000 / Forces container to run as Linux user ID 1000, This ensures: even if image defaults to root → Kubernetes overrides it

seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault:  Applies Linux system call filtering . This restricts dangerous system calls
like Firewall for Linux system calls

nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged: This is a pre-built non-root version of nginx

allowPrivilegeEscalation: false / Prevents container from gaining more privileges. Blocks setuid binaries and privilege escalation exploits

capabilities.drop: ["ALL"] / Linux capabilities control system-level permissions.

Dropping ALL means:
✔ No special Linux powers like:
NET_ADMIN
SYS_ADMIN
CHOWN
Container becomes very restricted

nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged / non-root user image good for Production.


I am using root user to run kubectl but in yml it says runAsNonRoot: true , how it works?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Your Linux user (root running kubectl) has nothing to do with the user inside the container.

They are two completely different layers in Kubernetes.
--------------------------------------------
1. Your machine (where you run kubectl) 2. Inside the container (Pod runtime)

1. Your machine (where you run kubectl)
-------------------

root user on EC2 / VM

This only means:

you have permission to talk to Kubernetes API
you can create/delete pods

✔ This is called cluster access

Inside the container (Pod runtime)
--------------------------
runAsNonRoot: true
runAsUser: 1000

This means:

what Linux user the container process runs as

✔ This is called container user

kubectl EC2 root with admin access → controls cluster  
runAsNonRoot → controls container

✔ kubectl authentication ≠ container privilege
✔ root on host ≠ root in container


Final Pod Behavior
---------------
Pod starts as UID 1000
No root access
No special Linux privileges
Seccomp filters applied

runAsNonRoot → No root  
runAsUser → Force safe user  
allowPrivilegeEscalation → No privilege boost  
capabilities.drop → No Linux admin powers  
seccomp → Syscall firewall  

WHY THIS WORKS (IMPORTANT)

This pod satisfies ALL restricted rules:
| Requirement              | Value          | Status                      |   |
| ------------------------ | -------------- | --------------------------- | - |
| runAsNonRoot             | true           | ✅                           |   |
| runAsUser                | 1000           | ✅                           |   |
| privileged               | not used       | ✅                           |   |
| allowPrivilegeEscalation | false          | ✅                           |   |
| capabilities             | ALL dropped    | ✅                           |   |
| seccomp                  | RuntimeDefault | ✅                           |   |
| image                    | non-root nginx | nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged | ✅ |


| Pod Type        | Result  |
| --------------- | ------- |
| privileged=true | Blocked |
| normal pod      | Allowed |


Restricted mode blocks:

privileged containers
host networking
host PID
root filesystem modifications
dangerous Linux capabilities


===================================
Till now we have seen Privileged and Restricted Policy , lets talk about Baseline Policy

2. Baseline Policy
------------------
Purpose: Prevents clearly dangerous configurations while still allowing common workloads.

Good for:

normal applications
dev/test environments
moderate security

What Baseline Blocks
--------------
❌ privileged containers
❌ host PID access
❌ host network access
❌ dangerous Linux capabilities

What Baseline Allows
---------------------
✅ Running as root (sometimes)

✅ Standard application containers

✅ Common workloads

Example:
-------
kubectl create ns dev
kubectl label ns dev pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=baseline


3. Restricted Policy
====================
Purpose: Provides strong security enforcement.

Good for:

production
multi-tenant clusters
enterprise environments

What Restricted Blocks
------------------------
Everything baseline blocks PLUS:

❌ running as root
❌ privilege escalation
❌ writable root filesystem
❌ unsafe capabilities
❌ insecure seccomp settings

Example:
--------
kubectl label ns prod pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted


| Policy     | Security Level |
| ---------- | -------------- |
| baseline   | Medium         |
| restricted | High           |

| Environment              | Recommended |
| ------------------------ | ----------- |
| Dev/Test                 | baseline    |
| Production               | restricted  |
| Highly secure enterprise | restricted  |


Interview Questions
-------------------
| Feature               | Privileged                | Baseline          | Restricted                    |
| --------------------- | ------------------------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------- |
| Security level        | ❌ No security             | ⚠️ Medium         | 🔒 Strict                     |
| Purpose               | Debug / trusted workloads | General workloads | Production / secure workloads |
| Root user allowed     | ✅ Yes                     | ⚠️ Sometimes      | ❌ No                          |
| Privileged containers | ✅ Allowed                 | ❌ Blocked         | ❌ Blocked                     |
| HostNetwork / HostPID | ✅ Allowed                 | ❌ Blocked         | ❌ Blocked                     |
| Privilege escalation  | ✅ Allowed                 | ❌ Limited         | ❌ Blocked                     |
| Linux capabilities    | ✅ Full                    | ⚠️ Limited        | ❌ Dropped (ALL)               |
| runAsNonRoot required | ❌ No                      | ❌ No              | ✅ Yes                         |
| seccomp required      | ❌ No                      | ❌ No              | ✅ RuntimeDefault              |
| Use case              | Debug, dev                | Dev/Test apps     | Production systems            |


Privileged → No restrictions, full access
Baseline → Blocks dangerous configs, allows normal apps
Restricted → Strong security enforcement, no root, no privileges



