What is Kyverno?
---------------

Kyverno (an admission controller)

Kyverno (“policy” in Greek) is a Kubernetes-native policy engine that runs as a validating + mutating admission controller.
It can:

Validate resources (block if they don’t meet rules)

Mutate resources (auto-add labels, annotations, etc.)


Install HELM
---------------
curl -fsSL -o get_helm.sh https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm/main/scripts/get-helm-3
chmod 700 get_helm.sh
./get_helm.sh
helm version


Install Kyverno
---------------

helm repo add kyverno https://kyverno.github.io/kyverno/
helm repo update
helm install kyverno kyverno/kyverno -n kyverno --create-namespace

kubectl get pods -n kyverno


vi require-env-label.yml

apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy
metadata:
  name: require-env-label
spec:
  validationFailureAction: Enforce
  rules:
    - name: check-env-label
      match:
        resources:
          kinds:
            - Pod
      validate:
        message: "Pod must have an 'env' label (e.g., env=dev, env=prod)"
        pattern:
          metadata:
            labels:
              env: "?*"

FYI
---
Warning: kyverno.io/v1 ClusterPolicy is deprecated and will be removed in a future release; migrate to ValidatingPolicy, MutatingPolicy, GeneratingPolicy or ImageValidatingPolicy (policies.kyverno.io), see https://kyverno.io/docs/guides/migration-to-cel/



kubectl apply -f require-env-label.yaml

kubectl run testpod --image=nginx    [get error]

kubectl run testpod --image=nginx --labels=env=dev   [success]

kubectl delete -f require-env-label.yaml

Another example:  Whenever a new Pod is created — Kyverno will automatically add the label: environment: production
--------------

vi add-label-policy.yml

apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy
metadata:
  name: add-default-label
spec:
  rules:
    - name: add-env-label
      match:
        resources:
          kinds:
            - Pod
      mutate:
        patchStrategicMerge:
          metadata:
            labels:
              environment: production

kubectl apply -f add-label-policy.yaml


Warning: kyverno.io/v1 ClusterPolicy is deprecated and will be removed in a future release; migrate to ValidatingPolicy, MutatingPolicy, GeneratingPolicy or ImageValidatingPolicy (policies.kyverno.io), see https://kyverno.io/docs/guides/migration-to-cel/
clusterpolicy.kyverno.io/add-default-label created




kubectl run nginx --image=nginx   [Create a pod]

kubectl get pod nginx -o yaml | grep environment   [this command will give output in yaml]
or
kubectl describe pod nginx | grep production

Workflow
-------

That label was automatically added by Kyverno before the Pod was admitted into the cluster.
When you run kubectl run nginx, the Admission Controller (Kyverno) intercepts the API request.
It checks your ClusterPolicy.
It modifies the Pod spec to inject the missing label.
Kubernetes then creates the modified Pod.

This is a mutation-type Admission Controller example (it changes requests).

1. What is an Admission Controller in Kubernetes?
--------------------------------------------------
Intercepts API requests after authentication/authorization
Validates or mutates objects before storing in etcd

At what stage do Admission Controllers work?
---------------------------------------
Authentication
→ Authorization
→ Admission Controller
→ etcd

Difference between Authentication, Authorization, and Admission Controller?

| Component            | Purpose                            |
| -------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| Authentication       | Who are you                        |
| Authorization        | What can you do                    |
| Admission Controller | Should request be modified/allowed |

Difference between Mutating and Validating Admission Controllers?

| Type       | Action                |
| ---------- | --------------------- |
| Mutating   | Modifies object       |
| Validating | Allows/denies request |

